content.text:
I added a home-page-only rule at the end of `site/css/pages.css` that sets the grey descriptive text to `#3d4650`:

```css
.page-home .lede,
.page-home .card p,
.page-home .muted {
  color: #3d4650;
}
```

It covers:
- the hero subtitle;
- the text in the feature and "Why Acme" cards;
- the newsletter blurb;
- the "We never share your address" note.

The other pages are unaffected, because the rule is scoped to `body.page-home`.

I did not change the stat captions ("orders shipped this year" and similar). They are grey too, but they read as labels rather than text under a heading. If you want them darker, add `.page-home .stat-label` to the selector list.

The functional tests pass (16 of 16). The visual tests also pass (12 of 12), including the home page at desktop and phone size. I expected the home screenshots to differ, since the text colour changed. The visual comparison may be tolerant enough to absorb a small colour shift like this one. I haven't looked at the rendered page, and I didn't update any approved screenshots.

STATUS: DONE

